# Understanding Laravel Eloquent Collections and Nested Loops

When working with data retrieved from a database in Laravel, it is common to encounter confusion regarding how `Eloquent` collections handle results versus single models. The specific error you encounteredâ`Undefined property: Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Collection::$sku`âstems from a fundamental misunderstanding of what the `get()` method returns and how array access works on Eloquent objects.

In your initial code snippet, you query products using a scope and limit the results:
```php
$product = Product::whereOwnerAndStatus($owner, 0)->take($count)->get();
```
The critical detail here is the `get()` method. Unlike `first()` or `find()`, which return a single model instance or null, `get()` always returns an `Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Collection`. Even if there is only one result in the database, this collection will contain exactly one item.

When you attempt to access `$product->sku`, you are trying to read a property directly from the Collection object itself. The Collection class does not have a property named `sku`. Instead, the `sku` data resides inside each model instance within that collection. To access the data of every item in the collection, you must iterate over the items themselves using `foreach ($product as $item)` or by calling `$product->all()`, which converts the collection into a plain array.

Your second attempt suggests that the structure might be nested (e.g., an array of arrays). However, Eloquent collections do not automatically wrap data in extra layers unless explicitly defined via relationships or custom accessors. If you received `Invalid argument supplied for foreach()` on `$items`, it implies that `$items` was either null or already a scalar value rather than an object. This usually happens when the underlying query returns nothing and you try to loop over the result of a failed access, or if you misunderstood the relationship mapping.

### Correcting the Loop Logic

To process the SKU data correctly, you need to iterate through each product model returned by the query. Assuming your `Product` model has a `sku` attribute (either as a column in the table or a defined accessor), the correct approach is:

```php
foreach ($product as $item) {
    // Accessing the SKU property on the specific model instance
    if (isset($item->sku)) {
        echo "SKU: " . $item->sku;

        // Or access via array syntax if using mass assignment guards
        // echo $item['sku']; 
    }
}
```

If your intention was to loop through a relationship where `sku` is a collection of items rather than a direct attribute, the syntax would differ. For example, if you have a relationship defined in the model like `$this->hasMany(Sku::class, 'product_id')`, you must explicitly call that relationship method:

```php
foreach ($product as $item) {
    foreach ($item->skus() as $skuModel) { // Note: relationship methods are typically plural
        // Process individual SKU model here
        echo $skuModel->name;
    }
}
```

### Debugging Data Structures

Before diving into complex loops, it is often helpful to inspect the raw data structure using `dd()` (if you have Laravel Debugbar) or `var_dump()`. This helps confirm whether `$product` is a single model, a collection of models, or an empty result.

```php
var_dump($product); 
// Expected Output: object(Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Collection)#x of ...
// Collection Items: array(1) { [0]=> object(Product)... }
```

If your database schema actually stores multiple SKUs in a single row (e.g., a JSON column or a comma-separated string), you must handle that serialization before looping. Eloquent does not automatically explode JSON strings into arrays without an accessor or relationship configuration.

### Best Practices for Controllers

When handling input like `$owner` and `$count`, always ensure the values are sanitized. In modern Laravel versions (5.8+), `Input::get()` is deprecated in favor of the `request()` helper. While your original code used Laravel 4 syntax, migrating to a newer version ensures better security practices regarding SQL injection when dealing with user input for queries.

If you are building an API or need to return this data as JSON, ensure that you are not exposing internal properties unintentionally. Using `$item->toArray()` converts the model into a simple array, which is safer for JSON responses than echoing model attributes directly.

For more advanced querying patterns or if you find yourself struggling with Eloquent relationships and scopes in complex ways, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) can provide deeper insights into specific Laravel versions and common architectural patterns used in high-performance applications.
